<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		Universal Links — the iOS counterpart to the autoVerify App Links
		intent-filter in android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml. Claims
		the same three path prefixes under https://opaqueshare.com/ so an
		email or share link opens the app when installed and falls back to
		the branded web page otherwise:

		  /r/*            — in-app link-mode receive (client ADR-0010;
		                    fallback: server-rendered decrypt page,
		                    server ADR-0035)
		  /password-reset — in-app "choose new password" form
		  /verify-email   — in-app auto-verify screen

		iOS has no per-prefix syntax here: the entitlement claims the
		HOST, and the path filtering happens in the
		apple-app-site-association (AASA) file the host serves. See
		infra/www/.well-known/apple-app-site-association in the server
		repo for the path list.

		OPERATOR TODO (both must be done or universal links silently fall
		back to opening Safari):
		  1. Enable the Associated Domains capability for
		     com.opaqueshare.app in the Apple Developer portal, then
		     regenerate the provisioning profile.
		  2. Replace TEAMID in the AASA file with the real Apple
		     Developer Team ID and deploy it. iOS fetches the AASA over
		     HTTPS at install time — it must return
		     `Content-Type: application/json` with no redirect.

		`applinks:` is intentionally the only entry. Do not add
		`webcredentials:` unless password autofill is actually wired up;
		an unbacked claim just adds a failing fetch at install time.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:opaqueshare.com</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
